16            2.7408     3.1416       2.7408      +0.1908

The ceiling moves by 0.407 in kh across orders 4 to 16, while the
half-spectral rule does not move at all. The observed pileup is
0.979 above pi/2, which is 98 per cent of the way from pi/2 to
the eighth-order ceiling, so the two hypotheses are far apart in
kh even though the numbers 2.573 and 1.571 look similar on a page.

CONCLUSION. The pileup at kh = 2.55 lies within 0.023 of the
eighth-order ceiling and 0.979 above the half-spectral limit. It is
the operator ceiling. A simulation at a different order would move
the pileup by the amounts tabulated above and is the confirming
experiment, but the arithmetic already separates the hypotheses:
pi/2 is not close to 2.55 by any measure.
